<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>DemoProducts.Api</RootNamespace>

    <PublishAot>true</PublishAot>
    <!-- Already implied by PublishAot; stated so the consequence is visible to the reader. -->
    <TrimMode>full</TrimMode>
    <EnableRequestDelegateGenerator>true</EnableRequestDelegateGenerator>

    <!-- Names the offending METHOD in the publish log instead of collapsing to one IL2104 per assembly. -->
    <TrimmerSingleWarn>false</TrimmerSingleWarn>

    <!--
      ADR 0001, option A. TreatWarningsAsErrors=true (Directory.Build.props) propagates into ILC, which
      then reports 139 trim/AOT diagnostics as ERRORS and stops the publish. Every one of them comes from
      a third-party assembly whose newest asset predates net10.0 - Newtonsoft.Json (via Apache.Avro),
      Confluent.SchemaRegistry, Confluent.Kafka - and NONE from DemoProducts.*; no pin resolves them at
      these versions.

      The demotion is scoped as narrowly as the tooling allows: this property, this project. It does NOT
      touch the C# compiler warnings, which stay errors, and it adds no <NoWarn> and no
      [UnconditionalSuppressMessage] - the diagnostics are still printed, counted and reviewable.

      It lives here rather than on the publish command line so that the Dockerfile, the README command
      and CI all get the same result from one source of truth. ADR 0001 carries the per-family
      reachability argument and the residual risk.
    -->
    <IlcTreatWarningsAsErrors>false</IlcTreatWarningsAsErrors>

    <!-- A type missing from the JSON context throws instead of quietly reflecting - on the CLR too. -->
    <JsonSerializerIsReflectionEnabledByDefault>false</JsonSerializerIsReflectionEnabledByDefault>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Confluent.SchemaRegistry's REST client serialises its own request and response DTOs with
    Newtonsoft.Json, which binds by reflection over property names. Under TrimMode=full nothing
    statically references those properties, ILC removes them, and JsonConvert.SerializeObject emits an
    empty object: the native binary POSTed a literal `{}` (2 bytes) to
    /subjects/product-created-value/versions and the registry answered `Empty schema; error code:
    42201`. On the CLR the same code returns 201, so the gap only exists on the published binary.

    Rooting the assembly keeps those members. It is deliberately blunt: the DTO set is internal to the
    package and changes between versions, so naming each type would be a list that silently rots. This
    is a root, not a suppression - the reflection now finds what it looks for.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <TrimmerRootAssembly Include="Confluent.SchemaRegistry" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\DemoProducts.Application\DemoProducts.Application.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\DemoProducts.Infrastructure\DemoProducts.Infrastructure.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Serilog.AspNetCore" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
